[0.000, "o", "                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Explaining segmentation at turn boundaries with overlap\u001b[39m                                       \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Token budgeting with boost for recent slices  \u001b[39m                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Parallel summarization with slice-specific prompts\u001b[39m                                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Stitching strategies (simple vs LLM merge)\u001b[39m                                                    \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m\u001b[39m                                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mFiles being modified:\u001b[39m                                                                           \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- /packages/coding-agent/compaction-new.md\u001b[39m                                                      \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m\u001b[39m                                                           "]
[0.000, "o", "                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mNext step: Add this concrete design to compaction-new.md\u001b[39m                                        \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;240;198;116m\u001b[1m### Edge Cases\u001b[22m\u001b[39m                                                                                    \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m1. \u001b[39m\u001b[1mSession too small\u001b[22m: If total < 2 slices worth, just do single summarization (current approach)  \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m2. \u001b[39m\u001b[1mVery long single turn\u001b[22m: If one user message + response exceeds slice size, keep it as its own   \r\r\n slice (don't split m"]
[0.000, "o", "id-turn)                                                                      \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m3. \u001b[39m\u001b[1mAbort handling\u001b[22m: If user aborts mid-compaction, use whatever slice summaries completed + keep   \r\r\n raw messages for incomplete slices                                                                \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m4. \u001b[39m\u001b[1mOverlap deduplication\u001b[22m: When stitching, the LLM merge step handles this, or we can track which  \r\r\n messages were in overlap and instruct slices to handle them differently                           \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;240;198;116m\u001b[1m### Cost/Benefit\u001b[22m\u001b[39m                                                                                  \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[1mCosts:\u001b[22m                                                                                            \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183"]
[0.000, "o", "m- \u001b[39mMore LLM calls (N slices + optional stitch = N+1 calls vs 1-2 currently)                        \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mSlightly more total tokens processed (overlaps counted twice)                                   \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[1mBenefits:\u001b[22m                                                                                         \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mParallelizable (wall-clock time similar to single call)                                         \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mBetter detail preservation in long sessions                                                     \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mRecent work gets more budget automatically                                                      \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mMore robust (one slice failing doesn't lose everything)                                         \r\r\n                                                                          "]
[0.000, "o", "                         \r\r\n Want me to add this to compaction-new.md or prototype the actual code?                            \r\r\n\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m                                                                                                   \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m \u001b[39mwhat's wrong with splitting mid turn? we basically do this with the turn prefix summary, no?\u001b[39m      \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m                                                                                                   \u001b[49m\r\r\n\r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mThe user is right to question this. Let me think about what we currently do:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                      \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mIn our current compaction.ts, we have the concept of \"split turn\" where:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                          \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m1. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mWe find a cut point that might be in the middle of a turn (at an assistant message, no"]
[0.000, "o", "t a user \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mmessage)\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                          \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m2. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mIf we're cutting mid-turn, we identify the turn start (the user message that began that turn)\u001b[39m\u001b[23m  \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m3. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mWe generate TWO summaries:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                     \r\r\n     \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mHistory summary: everything before the turn that contains the cut point\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                     \r\r\n     \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mTurn prefix summary: the beginning of the split turn (from user message to cut point)\u001b[39m\u001b[23m       \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mSo we DO split mid-turn already, and we handle it by summarizing the prefix of that turn          \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;12"]
[0.000, "o", "8;128mseparately.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                       \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mFor slice-based summarization, we could absolutely split mid-turn. The question is just how to    \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mhandle it:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                        \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m1. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mOption A: Like we do now - generate a \"prefix summary\" for the split portion\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m2. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mOption B: Just include incomplete turns at slice boundaries in the overlap, so both adjacent   \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mslices see them\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3"]
[0.000, "o", "8;2;138;190;183m3. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mOption C: Just cut wherever, and trust the per-slice summaries to capture what's relevant\u001b[39m\u001b[23m      \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mThe user is right that avoiding mid-turn splits is an artificial constraint. The overlap          \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mmechanism should handle continuity anyway.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                        \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mLet me acknowledge this and revise the thinking.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                  \r\r\n\r\r\n You're right. We already handle mid-turn splits with the turn prefix summary. The overlap in      \r\r\n slice-based approach serves the same purpose - ensuring context carries across the cut.           \r\r\n                                                                 "]
[0.000, "o", "                                  \r\r\n So actually, the slicing can be purely token-based:                                               \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mMessages: [u1, a1, u2, a2, u3, a3, u4, a4, u5, a5]\u001b[39m                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m          |---- ~30K tokens ----||---- ~30K tokens ----||-- rest --|\u001b[39m                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m              slice 0               slice 1              slice 2\u001b[39m                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m                            ^overlap^\u001b[39m                                                           \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n                           "]
[0.000, "o", "                                                                        \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n If a slice boundary falls mid-turn (say, in the middle of \u001b[38;2;138;190;183ma3\u001b[39m), the overlap ensures both slice 1   \r\r\n and slice 2 see enough of that turn to understand context. No special handling needed.            \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n This actually simplifies things:                                                                  \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```typescript\u001b[39m                                                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mfunction segmentByTokens(\u001b[39m                                                                       \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  messages: Message[], \u001b[39m           "]
[0.001, "o", "                                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  sliceTokens: number, \u001b[39m                                                                         \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  overlapTokens: number\u001b[39m                                                                         \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m): Message[][] {\u001b[39m                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  const slices: Message[][] = [];\u001b[39m                                                               \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  let start = 0;\u001b[39m                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  \u001b[39m                                                                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  while (start < messages.length) {\u001b[39m                                                             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    let tokens = 0;\u001b[39m                               "]
[0.000, "o", "                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    let end = start;\u001b[39m                                                                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    \u001b[39m                                                                                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    // Accumulate until we hit slice target\u001b[39m                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    while (end < messages.length && tokens < sliceTokens) {\u001b[39m                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      tokens += estimateTokens(messages[end]);\u001b[39m                                                  \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      end++;\u001b[39m                                                                                    \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    }\u001b[39m                                                                                           \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    \u001b[39m                                                              "]
[0.000, "o", "                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    slices.push(messages.slice(start, end));\u001b[39m                                                    \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    \u001b[39m                                                                                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    // Move start back by overlap amount for next slice\u001b[39m                                         \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    let overlapStart = end;\u001b[39m                                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    let overlapAcc = 0;\u001b[39m                                                                         \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    while (overlapStart > start && overlapAcc < overlapTokens) {\u001b[39m                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      overlapStart--;\u001b[39m                                                                           \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      overlapAcc += estimateTokens(messages[overlapStart]);\u001b[39m                       "]
[0.000, "o", "              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    }\u001b[39m                                                                                           \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    start = overlapStart;\u001b[39m                                                                       \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  }\u001b[39m                                                                                             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  \u001b[39m                                                                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  return slices;\u001b[39m                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m}\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n                       "]
[0.000, "o", "                                                                            \r\r\n The overlap naturally handles:                                                                    \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mMid-turn continuity                                                                             \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mTool calls and their results staying together (if overlap is big enough)                        \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mContext for understanding what came before                                                      \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n The per-slice prompt just says \"summarize what happened in this segment\" without caring about     \r\r\n turn boundaries. The stitching step deduplicates the overlapping content.                         \r\r\n\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m                                                                                                   \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m \u001b[39mor maybe we"]
[0.000, "o", " don'T parallelize but summarize one slice after the other, feeding the combined       \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m summaries into the next slice summarization?\u001b[39m                                                      \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m                                                                                                   \u001b[49m\r\r\n\r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mAh, that's an interesting approach! Sequential summarization where each slice summarization gets  \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mthe accumulated summary so far as context. This is like a \"rolling\" or \"incremental\"              \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128msummarization.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                    \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mBenefits:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                         \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m1. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mEach s"]
[0.000, "o", "lice summary can reference and build on the previous summary\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                             \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m2. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mNo deduplication needed at stitch time - each summary is already aware of what came before\u001b[39m\u001b[23m     \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m3. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mThe final slice's summary IS the complete summary (no stitching step)\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                          \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m4. \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mCan use overlap or not - the accumulated summary provides the continuity\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                       \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mIt's like:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                        \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mSlice 0 → Summary 0\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                             \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;1"]
[0.000, "o", "28mSlice 1 + Summary 0 → Summary 1 \u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mSlice 2 + Summary 1 → Summary 2\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                 \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128m...\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                             \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mSlice N + Summary N-1 → Final Summary\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                           \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mThis is actually more like how humans would summarize a long document - read a section, summarize \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mit, then read the next section with that summary in mind.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                         \r\r\n                                                                     "]
[0.000, "o", "                              \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mTrade-offs vs parallel:\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                           \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mSequential: No parallelization, but no stitch step, better coherence, each summary aware of     \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mcontext\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                           \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mParallel: Faster wall-clock time, but needs deduplication/stitching, summaries might overlap or \r\r\n \u001b[3;38;2;128;128;128mcontradict\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                        \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;128;128;128mThe sequential approach seems cleaner actually. Let me describe this.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                             \r\r\n\r\r\n That's a cleaner design. Each slice summarization h"]
[0.000, "o", "as context from what came before:              \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mSlice 0 messages          → Summary 0\u001b[39m                                                           \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mSlice 1 messages + Summary 0 → Summary 1  \u001b[39m                                                      \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mSlice 2 messages + Summary 1 → Summary 2\u001b[39m                                                        \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m...\u001b[39m                                                                                             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mSlice N messages + Summary N-1 → Final Summary\u001b[39m                                                  \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                "]
[0.000, "o", "               \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n Benefits:                                                                                         \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mNo stitching/deduplication step needed                                                          \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mEach summary builds on previous, naturally coherent                                             \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mNo overlap needed - the running summary IS the context                                          \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mFinal slice output is the complete summary                                                      \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;138;190;183m- \u001b[39mSummary grows incrementally, stays focused                                                      \r\r\n                                                                      "]
[0.000, "o", "                             \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```typescript\u001b[39m                                                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104masync function sequentialSliceCompaction(\u001b[39m                                                       \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  messages: Message[],\u001b[39m                                                                          \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  sliceTokens: number,\u001b[39m                                                                          \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  summaryBudget: number\u001b[39m                                                                         \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m): Promise<string> {\u001b[39m                                                                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  const slices = segmentByTokens(messages, sliceTokens);\u001b[39m                                        \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  let runningSummary = '';\u001b[39m                                                         "]
[0.000, "o", "             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  \u001b[39m                                                                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  for (let i = 0; i < slices.length; i++) {\u001b[39m                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    const isLast = i === slices.length - 1;\u001b[39m                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    \u001b[39m                                                                                            \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    runningSummary = await summarizeSlice({\u001b[39m                                                     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      messages: slices[i],\u001b[39m                                                                      \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      previousSummary: runningSummary,\u001b[39m                                                          \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      sliceIndex: i,\u001b[39m                                                                            \r\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      totalSlices: slices.length,\u001b[39m                                                               \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      isLast,\u001b[39m                                                                                   \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m      maxTokens: isLast ? summaryBudget : Math.floor(summaryBudget * 0.8),\u001b[39m                      \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m    });\u001b[39m                                                                                         \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  }\u001b[39m                                                                                             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  \u001b[39m                                                                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m  return runningSummary;\u001b[39m                                                                        \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m}\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;"]
[0.000, "o", "128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n The prompt for each slice:                                                                        \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m${previousSummary ? `Summary of earlier conversation:\\n${previousSummary}\\n\\n---\\n\\n` : ''}\u001b[39m     \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m\u001b[39m                                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mSummarize this segment (${sliceIndex + 1} of ${totalSlices}) of the coding session.\u001b[39m             \r\r\n  "]
[0.000, "o", " \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m${previousSummary ? 'Update and extend the summary above with information from this segment.' : \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m''}\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m${isLast ? 'This is the most recent activity - be detailed about current state and next steps.' \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m: ''}\u001b[39m                                                                                             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m\u001b[39m                                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104mFocus on:\u001b[39m                                                                                       \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- User requests and intent\u001b[39m                                                                      \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Files modified (with paths)\u001b[39m                                                                   \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Key "]
[0.000, "o", "decisions and their rationale\u001b[39m                                                             \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m- Errors and fixes\u001b[39m                                                                              \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m${isLast ? '- Exact current task and planned next steps' : ''}\u001b[39m                                  \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m\u001b[39m                                                                                                \r\r\n   \u001b[38;2;181;189;104m${isLast ? '' : 'Be concise - this summary will be extended with later segments.'}\u001b[39m              \r\r\n \u001b[38;2;128;128;128m```\u001b[39m                                                                                               \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n The key instruction for non-final slices: \"Update and extend the summary\" rather than \"crea"]
[0.000, "o", "te a   \r\r\n new summary\". This keeps it from ballooning - the model refines rather than appends.              \r\r\n                                                                                                   \r\r\n Downside: Sequential means total time = N × single-call time. But N is typically small (3-5       \r\r\n slices for a long session), and we avoid the complexity of merging.                               \r\r\n\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m                                                                                                   \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m \u001b[39mno we don'T want to update the summary, we want to generate a summary for the slice, telling the  \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m model: this is what happened before that, passing in all previous summaries possibly?\u001b[39m             \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m                                                                                                   \u001b[49m\r\r\n\u001b[48;2;52;53;65m \u001b[39mcan we set up a simple self-contained test in coding-agent/test (can be just a"]
